WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.600
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:00.600 --> 00:00:02.392
POLONG LIN: In this
video, we will show you

00:00:02.392 --> 00:00:04.800
how to build a serverless,
real-time credit card fraud

00:00:04.800 --> 00:00:07.620
detection solution
using Google Cloud.

00:00:07.620 --> 00:00:09.990
My name is Polong Lin,
a developer advocate

00:00:09.990 --> 00:00:11.220
for Google Cloud.

00:00:11.220 --> 00:00:14.340
And together with Quantiphi,
a GCP premier partner,

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:16.592
we co-developed a
design pattern to help

00:00:16.592 --> 00:00:18.300
you accelerate building
a fraud detection

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:19.680
solution with Google Cloud.

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:20.930
ESHAN TYAGI: I am Eshan Tyagi.

00:00:20.930 --> 00:00:23.070
I am a senior business
analyst at Quantiphi.

00:00:23.070 --> 00:00:24.612
PAVAN KATTAMURI:
I'm Pavan Kattamuri.

00:00:24.612 --> 00:00:26.790
And I'm a senior cloud
engineer at Quantiphi.

00:00:26.790 --> 00:00:29.190
POLONG LIN: Let's first
talk about fraud detection

00:00:29.190 --> 00:00:30.820
in the industry.

00:00:30.820 --> 00:00:32.302
What does Quantiphi see?

00:00:32.302 --> 00:00:34.260
ESHAN TYAGI: Fraud
detection is a major concern

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.960
for many businesses
and financial services.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:38.850
Global losses from
payment fraud have

00:00:38.850 --> 00:00:40.680
tripled in the last 10 years.

00:00:40.680 --> 00:00:43.560
In the UK alone,
for every 100 pounds

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:47.135
spent using credit cards,
7.5 pence was lost to fraud.

00:00:47.135 --> 00:00:48.510
PAVAN KATTAMURI:
So the challenge

00:00:48.510 --> 00:00:51.630
is to build and manage a
real-time fraud detection

00:00:51.630 --> 00:00:54.130
solution at scale.

00:00:54.130 --> 00:00:56.130
POLONG LIN: So in this
video, we will show you

00:00:56.130 --> 00:00:59.700
how to build a real-time fraud
detection solution broken down

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.520
into the following sections--
how to prepare the training

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:06.210
data in BigQuery; how to
build the fraud detection

00:01:06.210 --> 00:01:10.050
model directly within
BigQuery using BigQuery ML;

00:01:10.050 --> 00:01:12.750
how to build a pipeline
to do real-time model

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:16.570
predictions using
DataFlow; how to set up

00:01:16.570 --> 00:01:20.090
alert-based notifications
when fraud is detected using

00:01:20.090 --> 00:01:23.710
Pub/Sub; and how to create
interactive dashboards

00:01:23.710 --> 00:01:27.570
for business and technical
operations using Data Studio.

00:01:27.570 --> 00:01:29.320
ESHAN TYAGI: And the
code and instructions

00:01:29.320 --> 00:01:31.390
to follow this design
pattern are all linked

00:01:31.390 --> 00:01:33.077
in the video description below.

00:01:33.077 --> 00:01:35.410
POLONG LIN: Let's talk about
preparing the training data

00:01:35.410 --> 00:01:37.150
in BigQuery.

00:01:37.150 --> 00:01:39.820
ESHAN TYAGI: To detect if a
transaction is fraud or not,

00:01:39.820 --> 00:01:41.980
you want to have information
on the transaction

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:43.585
and the customer's information.

00:01:43.585 --> 00:01:47.320
So normally, these historical
records are in separate tables.

00:01:47.320 --> 00:01:49.870
You may have one table for
transactions and another

00:01:49.870 --> 00:01:52.930
to keep track of your customers'
demographic information.

00:01:52.930 --> 00:01:55.180
But to make it easier
to show you here,

00:01:55.180 --> 00:01:57.040
we have combined
both of the data sets

00:01:57.040 --> 00:01:58.810
into a single
table for training.

00:01:58.810 --> 00:02:00.310
PAVAN KATTAMURI:
This table is going

00:02:00.310 --> 00:02:03.700
to be our training data, where
each row is a transaction that

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:05.680
occurred in the past,
containing details

00:02:05.680 --> 00:02:08.949
of the transaction and
some customer information.

00:02:08.949 --> 00:02:11.380
Each column is a feature
such as what times

00:02:11.380 --> 00:02:14.780
the transaction occurred, what
the merchant was, category,

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:16.830
and so on.

00:02:16.830 --> 00:02:19.800
There is also a label
feature called is_fraud,

00:02:19.800 --> 00:02:22.250
which is a value of 1 or 0.

00:02:22.250 --> 00:02:24.680
This is what the model
will then try to predict--

00:02:24.680 --> 00:02:28.160
a probability of
fraud between 0 and 1.

00:02:28.160 --> 00:02:30.950
The data here is
simulated data, so you

00:02:30.950 --> 00:02:34.413
can replace this with your
own data for your needs.

00:02:34.413 --> 00:02:36.830
POLONG LIN: Could you elaborate
on your choice of BigQuery

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:37.400
here?

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.130
ESHAN TYAGI: It's an extremely
scalable data warehouse.

00:02:40.130 --> 00:02:42.360
It's fast and easy to use.

00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:44.450
And we find that
customers choose BigQuery

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.970
because you get the best
of enterprise-grade data

00:02:46.970 --> 00:02:51.210
storage, data analytics, machine
learning, all in one place.

00:02:51.210 --> 00:02:54.110
We have customers storing
terabytes of data in BigQuery,

00:02:54.110 --> 00:02:57.380
and they have the capability to
train machine learning models

00:02:57.380 --> 00:02:58.937
within BigQuery directly.

00:02:58.937 --> 00:03:00.770
POLONG LIN: So speaking
of machine learning,

00:03:00.770 --> 00:03:03.740
the next step is building
the machine learning model

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:07.040
to detect fraud
using BigQuery ML.

00:03:07.040 --> 00:03:09.500
Perhaps the magic
of BigQuery ML is

00:03:09.500 --> 00:03:12.590
that, without needing to move
your data out of BigQuery,

00:03:12.590 --> 00:03:15.260
using BigQuery ML, you can
train and deploy machine

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:17.930
learning models
directly using SQL.

00:03:17.930 --> 00:03:21.620
And you don't have to procure
or maintain extra infrastructure

00:03:21.620 --> 00:03:24.500
because it uses BigQuery's
built-in autoscaling

00:03:24.500 --> 00:03:26.690
infrastructure to
handle model training

00:03:26.690 --> 00:03:28.525
and deployment at scale.

00:03:28.525 --> 00:03:29.900
ESHAN TYAGI: And
at Quantiphi, we

00:03:29.900 --> 00:03:31.880
find our customers
really enjoy being

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.790
able to use the democratized
ML capabilities, which

00:03:34.790 --> 00:03:38.420
allow analysts to run machine
learning models using SQL.

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:40.130
So with the training
data, we can

00:03:40.130 --> 00:03:43.370
train a model using just
SQL with BigQuery ML.

00:03:43.370 --> 00:03:46.070
While there are several
classification algorithms

00:03:46.070 --> 00:03:48.460
available to choose
from with BigQuery

00:03:48.460 --> 00:03:51.440
ML, like logistic regression,
deep neural networks,

00:03:51.440 --> 00:03:55.190
and AutoML tables, we find that
XGBoost models worked really

00:03:55.190 --> 00:03:58.310
well with minimal
hyperparameter tuning required,

00:03:58.310 --> 00:04:00.840
making it easy to build
good models quickly.

00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:02.840
PAVAN KATTAMURI: So in
the Create Model segment,

00:04:02.840 --> 00:04:06.350
we give the model a name, like
simplemodel, set the model

00:04:06.350 --> 00:04:09.710
type to BOOSTED_TREE_CLASSIFIER
for XGBoost,

00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:13.940
set the input level calls to
is_fraud as the level of 0

00:04:13.940 --> 00:04:17.519
and 1 that we want to predict,
and then select the training

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.200
data.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.720
You can also adjust
some hyperparameters

00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:25.215
such as num_parallel_tree and
max_iterations for example.

00:04:25.215 --> 00:04:27.090
ESHAN TYAGI: We were
able to train this model

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:30.000
within just 10 minutes
over 1.3 million rows

00:04:30.000 --> 00:04:32.940
and 10 features, which is
really fast considering

00:04:32.940 --> 00:04:35.310
there is no need to set
up extra infrastructure

00:04:35.310 --> 00:04:36.430
to train the models.

00:04:36.430 --> 00:04:37.847
POLONG LIN: Now,
I notice that you

00:04:37.847 --> 00:04:39.450
called the model "simplemodel."

00:04:39.450 --> 00:04:41.440
Could you elaborate
on that a little bit?

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:42.180
ESHAN TYAGI: Yes.

00:04:42.180 --> 00:04:44.820
In practice, just looking
at the transactional data

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:48.010
and the customer's demographics,
like what country they are in

00:04:48.010 --> 00:04:50.280
and their age, is
actually very likely

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:53.130
to be insufficient to produce
a strong performing fraud

00:04:53.130 --> 00:04:54.033
detection model.

00:04:54.033 --> 00:04:55.950
POLONG LIN: So what would
you recommend, then,

00:04:55.950 --> 00:04:58.480
to build a good fraud
detection model?

00:04:58.480 --> 00:05:00.370
ESHAN TYAGI: Well,
there are many ways.

00:05:00.370 --> 00:05:03.210
One way which we can show
in this design pattern

00:05:03.210 --> 00:05:05.020
is to do more
feature engineering

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.190
by also looking at the
customer's historical activity.

00:05:08.190 --> 00:05:10.660
If someone typically
spends on average

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:13.050
$100 per month on
their credit card,

00:05:13.050 --> 00:05:16.200
and this transaction shows
you are spending $2,000,

00:05:16.200 --> 00:05:18.010
then that might be unusual.

00:05:18.010 --> 00:05:20.820
So you would want the
model to pick up on this

00:05:20.820 --> 00:05:22.840
by adding in relevant features.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:25.350
PAVAN KATTAMURI: And this is
perhaps just a starting point.

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:27.930
In reality, you may want to
have a lot more features,

00:05:27.930 --> 00:05:31.060
and you may want to improve
your model in other ways,

00:05:31.060 --> 00:05:33.700
like model selection,
hyperparameter tuning,

00:05:33.700 --> 00:05:37.680
and model ensembling, which had
all possible with BigQuery ML.

00:05:37.680 --> 00:05:39.930
Because there are so
many approaches depending

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:43.300
on the data available and their
exact scenario, in this design

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:44.880
pattern, we just
show you how you

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:47.640
can get started with a few
extra aggregate features

00:05:47.640 --> 00:05:50.910
of the customer activity,
such as how long it's

00:05:50.910 --> 00:05:53.160
been since their
last transaction;

00:05:53.160 --> 00:05:56.430
the average spent on the credit
card per week or per month;

00:05:56.430 --> 00:05:59.190
and also, the number of
transactions of average

00:05:59.190 --> 00:06:03.118
in the last 24 hours, just as
examples of what you could do.

00:06:03.118 --> 00:06:05.160
POLONG LIN: With the models
built in BigQuery ML,

00:06:05.160 --> 00:06:07.790
could you show us how
you evaluate the models?

00:06:07.790 --> 00:06:10.290
PAVAN KATTAMURI: You can see
the evaluation metrics directly

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:13.260
on the BigQuery UI under
the Evaluation tab.

00:06:13.260 --> 00:06:18.180
You can see the log loss,
ROC AUC, precision, recall,

00:06:18.180 --> 00:06:22.140
accuracy, F1 score, and
other visualizations as well.

00:06:22.140 --> 00:06:24.900
To actually compare F1
score across models,

00:06:24.900 --> 00:06:29.655
you can use SELECT * from and
ML.EVALUATE and then the model

00:06:29.655 --> 00:06:30.300
name.

00:06:30.300 --> 00:06:32.400
You can do this
for both the models

00:06:32.400 --> 00:06:35.600
and combine the results
using UNION ALL.

00:06:35.600 --> 00:06:37.100
ESHAN TYAGI: To
compare metrics, you

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:40.310
can use F1 score
and ROC AUC, where

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:44.000
the higher the values are,
the better the model performs.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:46.730
For imbalanced data sets
like in credit card fraud,

00:06:46.730 --> 00:06:49.250
where you have mostly
normal transactions,

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.580
then we recommend using the F1
score to compare the models.

00:06:52.580 --> 00:06:54.650
And indeed, if you
look at the model that

00:06:54.650 --> 00:06:57.890
uses aggregate behavioral
data, the F1 score

00:06:57.890 --> 00:07:02.250
is 0.78 versus the simple
model's F1 score of 0.56.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:05.360
So it's improved
dramatically by incorporating

00:07:05.360 --> 00:07:06.680
the historical data.

00:07:06.680 --> 00:07:08.780
Although there is room
for model improvement

00:07:08.780 --> 00:07:11.900
as discussed earlier, we'll
just use the second model

00:07:11.900 --> 00:07:14.270
to demonstrate how you
can use a BigQuery ML

00:07:14.270 --> 00:07:16.910
model like this one in
a streaming pipeline

00:07:16.910 --> 00:07:18.625
for real-time fraud detection.

00:07:18.625 --> 00:07:20.000
POLONG LIN: With
the model built,

00:07:20.000 --> 00:07:23.090
let's talk about making
predictions to detect fraud.

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:27.770
Now, for predictions, there is
batch and online predictions.

00:07:27.770 --> 00:07:29.750
Could you elaborate
on the differences?

00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:30.560
ESHAN TYAGI: Sure.

00:07:30.560 --> 00:07:32.270
For batch predictions,
this is when

00:07:32.270 --> 00:07:35.480
you want to make predictions
on lots of data in bulk

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:39.320
at a time, which you can do
in BigQuery ML with SELECT *

00:07:39.320 --> 00:07:42.680
from ML.PREDICT your
model and the data you

00:07:42.680 --> 00:07:44.600
want to make predictions on.

00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:46.370
Batch predictions
are useful in making

00:07:46.370 --> 00:07:48.740
large amounts of predictions
in a single shot,

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:51.230
and you can do this
with BigQuery ML.

00:07:51.230 --> 00:07:53.900
But they are not optimized
for real-time inference,

00:07:53.900 --> 00:07:55.550
where you may have
lots of predictions

00:07:55.550 --> 00:07:56.960
you need to do per second.

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.460
PAVAN KATTAMURI: So that's where
online prediction comes in,

00:07:59.460 --> 00:08:00.950
which is for
real-time predictions

00:08:00.950 --> 00:08:03.020
in low-latency
situations for higher

00:08:03.020 --> 00:08:04.640
frequencies of operations.

00:08:04.640 --> 00:08:06.290
To do online
predictions, you will

00:08:06.290 --> 00:08:08.560
need to export and
host a BigQuery ML

00:08:08.560 --> 00:08:10.498
model on AI Platform.

00:08:10.498 --> 00:08:12.290
POLONG LIN: Another
way to export the model

00:08:12.290 --> 00:08:15.320
for online predictions
is, instead of hosting

00:08:15.320 --> 00:08:18.380
the model on AI Platform
as its own endpoint,

00:08:18.380 --> 00:08:20.900
you can similarly export
to Google Cloud Storage,

00:08:20.900 --> 00:08:24.280
but serve it directly
in a DataFlow pipeline.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:26.920
This may be helpful if you
want to optimize for lower

00:08:26.920 --> 00:08:30.790
average latency, but it may
also make model maintainability

00:08:30.790 --> 00:08:32.390
a little bit more difficult.

00:08:32.390 --> 00:08:34.299
So in this design
pattern, we'll just

00:08:34.299 --> 00:08:37.510
show you how the model is
hosted as its own endpoint

00:08:37.510 --> 00:08:40.659
on AI Platform for
online predictions.

00:08:40.659 --> 00:08:43.159
So Pavan, how does that work?

00:08:43.159 --> 00:08:45.660
PAVAN KATTAMURI: You can
easily do it via two steps.

00:08:45.660 --> 00:08:49.160
Step one, export the model
to a Cloud Storage location

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:51.950
by running bq extract
command with the model

00:08:51.950 --> 00:08:56.370
name and the destination
location as arguments.

00:08:56.370 --> 00:08:58.830
Step two, once it's
in Cloud Storage,

00:08:58.830 --> 00:09:01.560
you will need to create a
model on AI Platform which

00:09:01.560 --> 00:09:04.600
can hold one or more
versions of the model.

00:09:04.600 --> 00:09:06.630
So the next step is to
create a model version

00:09:06.630 --> 00:09:09.850
by running the gcloud
ai-platform versions create

00:09:09.850 --> 00:09:13.240
command, providing the GCS
location of the previously

00:09:13.240 --> 00:09:15.140
exported model from BigQuery ML.

00:09:17.800 --> 00:09:19.550
ESHAN TYAGI: And then
to make predictions,

00:09:19.550 --> 00:09:22.300
you can send a request to
the deployed model using

00:09:22.300 --> 00:09:25.190
the gcloud ai-platform
predict command by passing

00:09:25.190 --> 00:09:26.230
in the transaction data.

00:09:28.588 --> 00:09:30.130
POLONG LIN: So with
the model hosted,

00:09:30.130 --> 00:09:32.110
the next step is
putting it together

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:36.040
into a pipeline that can handle
transactions in real time,

00:09:36.040 --> 00:09:40.330
make predictions, and then send
notification alerts for fraud.

00:09:40.330 --> 00:09:41.830
ESHAN TYAGI: To
create the pipeline,

00:09:41.830 --> 00:09:43.480
you can build a
DataFlow pipeline

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:45.260
with the following elements.

00:09:45.260 --> 00:09:47.410
First, it ingests
new transactions

00:09:47.410 --> 00:09:49.240
from a Pub/Sub subscription.

00:09:49.240 --> 00:09:51.670
Pub/Sub is an event
messaging service.

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:53.530
So the Pub/Sub
subscription is waiting

00:09:53.530 --> 00:09:56.980
for any new transactions to
come in as essentially JSON

00:09:56.980 --> 00:09:57.970
messages.

00:09:57.970 --> 00:09:59.890
As a new transaction
is ingested,

00:09:59.890 --> 00:10:02.500
DataFlow then does a
lookup on the customer's

00:10:02.500 --> 00:10:05.770
historical activity to
do some aggregations.

00:10:05.770 --> 00:10:08.920
The lookups can be stored on
something like Firestore, which

00:10:08.920 --> 00:10:11.080
is a serverless NoSQL database.

00:10:11.080 --> 00:10:14.320
With the combined data,
DataFlow then invokes

00:10:14.320 --> 00:10:16.840
the fraud detection
model on AI Platform

00:10:16.840 --> 00:10:19.750
and gets a response with the
probability that it's fraud,

00:10:19.750 --> 00:10:21.910
between 0 and 1.

00:10:21.910 --> 00:10:24.610
The prediction outputs are
then stored in a separate table

00:10:24.610 --> 00:10:25.660
in BigQuery.

00:10:25.660 --> 00:10:28.870
And in parallel, DataFlow
also triggers a notification

00:10:28.870 --> 00:10:31.390
to a Pub/Sub topic,
which can then trigger

00:10:31.390 --> 00:10:33.053
any downstream notifications.

00:10:33.053 --> 00:10:35.470
POLONG LIN: When it comes to
detecting fraud in real time,

00:10:35.470 --> 00:10:38.800
what is the latency that
you get with this pipeline

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.610
from the incoming transaction
to the notification?

00:10:42.610 --> 00:10:44.650
And what do you typically
see your customers

00:10:44.650 --> 00:10:47.405
asking for in terms of
their required latency?

00:10:47.405 --> 00:10:49.030
ESHAN TYAGI: For some
of our customers,

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:52.540
the detection has to typically
occur within seconds for it

00:10:52.540 --> 00:10:53.560
to be effective.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:56.860
In this pipeline, the latency
between when a transaction

00:10:56.860 --> 00:10:59.410
occurs and when the fraud
notification is sent

00:10:59.410 --> 00:11:01.480
is around two to
eight seconds, which

00:11:01.480 --> 00:11:04.337
falls in line with what we
see institutions asking for.

00:11:04.337 --> 00:11:05.920
POLONG LIN: And what
if customers need

00:11:05.920 --> 00:11:07.750
much lower latency?

00:11:07.750 --> 00:11:09.820
ESHAN TYAGI: The
first way is, instead

00:11:09.820 --> 00:11:13.210
of hosting the model as an
endpoint on AI Platform,

00:11:13.210 --> 00:11:16.060
you can see decreases
in latency by exporting

00:11:16.060 --> 00:11:18.070
the model in Google
Cloud Storage

00:11:18.070 --> 00:11:21.280
and serve the predictions
directly from DataFlow,

00:11:21.280 --> 00:11:23.510
as mentioned earlier.

00:11:23.510 --> 00:11:26.650
However, if you still want
to keep the model endpoint

00:11:26.650 --> 00:11:29.950
as is on AI Platform,
then you can

00:11:29.950 --> 00:11:33.670
try to upgrade the machine type,
increasing the number of nodes

00:11:33.670 --> 00:11:37.870
for model serving, or increasing
the quota limit on AI Platform

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:39.050
predictions.

00:11:39.050 --> 00:11:40.840
POLONG LIN: And
what about DataFlow?

00:11:40.840 --> 00:11:43.360
PAVAN KATTAMURI: We find that
[INAUDIBLE] is really easy

00:11:43.360 --> 00:11:45.010
to use and scalable.

00:11:45.010 --> 00:11:47.298
It can autoscale
on its own, so you

00:11:47.298 --> 00:11:49.840
don't need to worry about the
streaming infrastructure needed

00:11:49.840 --> 00:11:51.460
to run these patterns.

00:11:51.460 --> 00:11:53.375
POLONG LIN: Could you
walk us through a demo?

00:11:53.375 --> 00:11:54.500
PAVAN KATTAMURI: Certainly.

00:11:54.500 --> 00:11:57.760
Let me show you how this works
on Google Cloud Platform,

00:11:57.760 --> 00:12:00.482
starting with ingesting
the transactions.

00:12:00.482 --> 00:12:02.920
First, let's verify that
we have a live stream

00:12:02.920 --> 00:12:05.440
of new messages
coming in via Pub/Sub

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:07.630
as real-time transactions.

00:12:07.630 --> 00:12:09.520
You can view some of
these transactions

00:12:09.520 --> 00:12:12.930
by clicking on
New Messages here.

00:12:12.930 --> 00:12:15.810
As you can see, each message
holds transaction details

00:12:15.810 --> 00:12:19.380
like the transaction date,
amount, merchant information,

00:12:19.380 --> 00:12:22.162
and user demographics
required for the ML model

00:12:22.162 --> 00:12:24.148
to do a prediction.

00:12:24.148 --> 00:12:27.380
But where is the aggregate
data of customer activity?

00:12:27.380 --> 00:12:30.740
Since aggregate data relies
on historical activity,

00:12:30.740 --> 00:12:34.390
it should be computed
on the fly in real time.

00:12:34.390 --> 00:12:37.000
Let's check that on Firestore.

00:12:37.000 --> 00:12:41.500
We are using Firestore to store
the users' recent transactions

00:12:41.500 --> 00:12:43.870
and look these up very quickly.

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:47.080
To compute attributes on
their recent activity,

00:12:47.080 --> 00:12:50.380
the DataFlow pipeline will
run these aggregations

00:12:50.380 --> 00:12:53.630
for the user, for example,
calculating volume

00:12:53.630 --> 00:12:56.660
or transaction
frequency, and then

00:12:56.660 --> 00:13:01.240
add that to the raw transaction
data we saw from Pub/Sub.

00:13:01.240 --> 00:13:03.730
At the same time,
DataFlow will also

00:13:03.730 --> 00:13:06.640
update this Firestore
record to only maintain

00:13:06.640 --> 00:13:09.280
the most recent
transactions required

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:12.440
for future computations.

00:13:12.440 --> 00:13:15.840
So let's take a look at
the DataFlow pipeline.

00:13:15.840 --> 00:13:18.350
This is our streaming
DataFlow pipeline,

00:13:18.350 --> 00:13:21.620
consuming real-time
transactions from Pub/Sub,

00:13:21.620 --> 00:13:24.150
converting them
into JSON format,

00:13:24.150 --> 00:13:28.510
doing a lookup to Firestore to
fetch the historical activity,

00:13:28.510 --> 00:13:32.270
computes aggregations,
invokes ML models

00:13:32.270 --> 00:13:36.380
deployed on AI Platform to
get the probability of fraud.

00:13:36.380 --> 00:13:38.540
And once the
prediction is done, it

00:13:38.540 --> 00:13:41.910
will send alert notifications
automatically to Pub/Sub

00:13:41.910 --> 00:13:44.900
so then downstream
processes can be integrated.

00:13:44.900 --> 00:13:48.200
At the same time, it also
stores output predictions

00:13:48.200 --> 00:13:51.750
from the fraud detection
model into a BigQuery table.

00:13:51.750 --> 00:13:55.590
So how does the output
prediction look like?

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:58.170
The column
is_fraud_model_w_aggregates

00:13:58.170 --> 00:14:01.100
is the predicted
output with value 1

00:14:01.100 --> 00:14:04.202
being a fraud transaction
and 0 being a genuine one.

00:14:04.202 --> 00:14:05.910
POLONG LIN: Notice
how the number of rows

00:14:05.910 --> 00:14:10.080
here increases from
12,000 to almost 13,000

00:14:10.080 --> 00:14:12.330
as new transactions
are processed and then

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.770
stored here in BigQuery.

00:14:14.770 --> 00:14:17.005
So Eshan, what's the next step?

00:14:17.005 --> 00:14:18.880
ESHAN TYAGI: Keeping an
eye on how everything

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:22.250
is working using dashboards is
really useful at this point.

00:14:22.250 --> 00:14:25.910
So first, here's a dashboard
we created for business users.

00:14:25.910 --> 00:14:28.120
POLONG LIN: So as
a business user,

00:14:28.120 --> 00:14:31.030
I may ask a question of,
where are all the fraud

00:14:31.030 --> 00:14:32.208
transactions occurring?

00:14:32.208 --> 00:14:34.750
PAVAN KATTAMURI: You can see it
broken down by merchant type.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:37.570
You can also see it
broken down by geography.

00:14:37.570 --> 00:14:39.460
And you can even click
on specific parts

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:41.530
on the map and filter
for transactions

00:14:41.530 --> 00:14:43.210
only in the state of Illinois.

00:14:43.210 --> 00:14:44.710
POLONG LIN: And
another question may

00:14:44.710 --> 00:14:48.255
be, how is the rate of fraud
detection changing over time?

00:14:48.255 --> 00:14:49.630
ESHAN TYAGI: In
these graphs, you

00:14:49.630 --> 00:14:51.400
can see the fraudulent
transactions

00:14:51.400 --> 00:14:54.550
over a period of time and
identify the average fraud

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:57.010
percentage on specific days.

00:14:57.010 --> 00:15:00.310
Next, we also created this
dashboard for a technical team

00:15:00.310 --> 00:15:02.590
to monitor the health
of the pipeline,

00:15:02.590 --> 00:15:05.800
track the incoming
transactions, monitor the output

00:15:05.800 --> 00:15:09.130
from individual components,
and supervise the output

00:15:09.130 --> 00:15:10.510
notification channel.

00:15:10.510 --> 00:15:13.990
POLONG LIN: And what data is
this dashboard built off of?

00:15:13.990 --> 00:15:15.550
ESHAN TYAGI: This
dashboard uses data

00:15:15.550 --> 00:15:20.140
from Cloud Operations, which
collects metrics, logs,

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:24.010
and traces across Google
Cloud and other applications.

00:15:24.010 --> 00:15:26.470
By visualizing this
data in a dashboard,

00:15:26.470 --> 00:15:29.710
it allows the technical user
to monitor model latency

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:32.170
as well as the volume of
fraud notifications going

00:15:32.170 --> 00:15:34.060
through DataFlow and Pub/Sub.

00:15:34.060 --> 00:15:35.950
PAVAN KATTAMURI: If
there are unusual peaks,

00:15:35.950 --> 00:15:39.820
or any line seems to be dropping
to 0 when they shouldn't, then

00:15:39.820 --> 00:15:41.740
that would tell us
that something perhaps

00:15:41.740 --> 00:15:45.060
is wrong that the technical
team needs to address.

00:15:45.060 --> 00:15:47.560
POLONG LIN: So if you'd like
to know more about the solution

00:15:47.560 --> 00:15:50.170
or want to see the
code, follow the links

00:15:50.170 --> 00:15:53.620
in the video description to
the public code repository.

00:15:53.620 --> 00:15:56.860
And with that, now you know how
to build a real-time, credit

00:15:56.860 --> 00:16:00.250
card fraud detection
solution on Google Cloud.

00:16:00.250 --> 00:16:04.120
Thank you, Quantiphi, for your
